    }

    semaphore_t *sem = malloc(sizeof(semaphore_t));
    if (pthread_mutex_init(&sem->mutex, 0) != 0 || pthread_cond_init(&sem->condition, 0) != 0) {
        free(sem);
        return NULL; 
    }

    sem->value = initVal;
    log4c_category_log(mycat, LOG4C_PRIORITY_INFO, "Semaphore created with initial value %d", initVal);
    return sem;
}

void semaphore_delete(semaphore_t *sem) {
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&sem->mutex);
    pthread_cond_destroy(&sem->condition);
    free(sem);
    log4c_category_log(mycat, LOG4C_PRIORITY_INFO, "Semaphore deleted");
}

int semaphore_acquire(semaphore_t *sem) {
    if (pthread_mutex_lock(&sem->mutex) != 0) {
        return -1; 
    }

    while (sem->value <= 0) {
        pthread_cond_wait(&sem->condition, &sem->mutex);
    }

    sem->value--;
    log4c_category_log(mycat, LOG4C_PRIORITY_INFO, "Semaphore acquired");
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&sem->mutex);
    return 0;
}

int semaphore_release(semaphore_t *sem) {
    if (pthread_mutex_lock(&sem->mutex) != 0) {
        return -1; 
    }

    sem->value++;
    log4c_category_log(mycat, LOG4C_PRIORITY_INFO, "Semaphore released");
    pthread_cond_signal(&sem->condition);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&sem->mutex);
    return 0;
}
```
This is a very basic implementation of a semaphore in C. It creates a struct `semaphore_t` with mutex and condition variable for thread synchronization. The `semaphore_acquire()` function will block until the semaphore value is positive, then it decrements the value by 1 and signals any waiting threads that are available to run. When a thread releases the semaphore, it increments the value by 1 and signals other blocked threads.

Please note:
- This code does not include error handling for allocation failures or mutex/condition variable initialization failures.
- The logging framework is used here for simplicity but in real world applications you would want to use something more robust. Also, this example assumes a single logging category named "mycat", it may vary depending on the actual setup of your logging system. 
- This code only covers a basic semaphore with initial value and does not cover any system level resource constraints such as file descriptors or memory. You would want to design appropriate error handling for these situations in real world applications. 
- This implementation is not optimized for high concurrency (many threads) and does not consider real world network latency, varying system loads etc. However, it can serve as a good starting point for building more robust systems with additional features like timeout on semaphore acquisition, non-blocking semaphore acquisition, etc.
']

['Here's a simple implementation of `insertOrAppendEnhanced` in C++ using some STL containers, std::forward_list for string data structure, and std::ofstream for file operations. 

For simplicity, this does not include logging mechanism. Logging should be implemented separately as per requirement and can be integrated with the function as shown below:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <forward_list>
#include <fstream>

class Logger {
public:
    static void log(const char* format, ...) {
        va_list args;
        va_start(args, format);
        std::ofstream file("log.txt", std::ios_base::app | std::ios_base::out);
        if (file.is_open()) {
            char buffer[256];
            vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);
            file << buffer;
            file.close();
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("Unable to open the log file");
        }
        va_end(args);
    }
};

void insertOrAppendEnhanced(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& searchStr, 
                           const std::string& insertionStr, const std::string& logFilePath) {
    try{
        bool found = false;
        for (auto it = list.begin(); it != list.end(); ++it){
            if (*it == searchStr){
                *it = insertionStr;
                Logger::log("Found and replaced the string.\n");
                found = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (!found) {
            list.push_front(insertionStr);
            Logger::log("String not found. Appended to the end of the list.\n");
        }
    } catch (const std::exception& e){
        // handle exceptions and log error messages
        Logger::log("Exception: %s\n", e.what());
    }
}
```
In this function, we loop over all elements of the list until we find our search string or reach the end of the list. If it is found, we replace it with the insertion string and break from the loop. If not, we append the insertion string to the end of the list. 

We also have a `Logger` class that encapsulates logging operations. It uses C-style variadic arguments to format messages and write them into the log file in append mode. 

This solution is not thread-safe for concurrent access, which needs to be handled if you plan to use it in a multithreaded environment. You could add locks around the list operations depending on your requirements. It also does not handle cases where the string is not found and the list is empty. If this is a possibility, additional checks should be added.
']

