['Here is a simplified version of how this could be implemented in Rust using the Hyper HTTP library, Tokio runtime, Futures library, and Serde for serialization. Note that this is a vast oversimplification of what would require to fulfil all requirements. It's better to break down the task into smaller parts and then work on each part separately:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use hyper::rt::{self, Future};
use hyper::service::{make_service_fn, service_fn};
use hyper::{Body, Request, Response, StatusCode};
use futures::stream::StreamExt;
use tokio::net::TcpListener;
use serde::{Serialize, Deserialize};

#[derive(Deserialize)]
struct Params {
    url: String,
    headers: HashMap<String, String>,
    method: Method,
    body: Option<Body>,
}

enum Method {
    GET,
    POST,
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let addr = "127.0.0.1:3000".parse().unwrap();
    let listener = TcpListener::bind(addr).await.unwrap();

    rt::run(listener.incoming().for_each(|socket| {
        rt::spawn(service_fn![service, socket]);
        Ok(())
    }));
}

async fn service(_req: Request<Body>) -> Result<Response<Body>, hyper::Error> {
    let params: Params = serde_json::from_slice(&body)?;
    match params.method {
        Method::GET => get(params),
        Method::POST => post(params),
    }
}

async fn get(params: Params) -> Result<Response<Body>, hyper::Error> {
    let client = reqwest::Client::new();
    let response = client.get(&params.url)
        .headers(params.headers)
        .send()
        .await?;
    Response::builder()
        .status(StatusCode::OK)
        .body(Body::from(response.text().unwrap()))
        .unwrap()
}

async fn post(params: Params) -> Result<Response<Body>, hyper::Error> {
    let client = reqwest::Client::new();
    let response = client.post(&params.url)
        .headers(params.headers)
        .body(params.body.unwrap())
        .send()
        .await?;
    Response::builder()
        .status(StatusCode::OK)
        .body(Body::from(response.text().unwrap()))
        .unwrap()
}
```

This code creates a simple HTTP client that listens for incoming connections on 127.0.0.1:3000 and then spawns a new task to handle each connection. Each request is expected to be a JSON object with the URL, headers, method, and body (if applicable). The client will then send the appropriate GET or POST request using reqwest and respond with the received response.

This code does not include error handling, logging, retries, rate limiting, encryption, or integration with external services. Each of these would require a separate implementation which is beyond the scope of this question. This code also doesn't handle authentication or dynamic token refreshing as these would involve integrating with OAuth2 servers and managing tokens securely.
