['Here's a very basic design for the `DataFetchingEngine` C++ library module:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <thread>
#include <future>
#include <chrono>
#include <cpr/cpr.h> // A simple HTTP client for C++, used for API calls
#include "rapidjson/document.h" // JSON parsing library 

class DataFetchingEngine {
public:
    DataFetchingEngine() {}

    ~DataFetchingEngine() {
        if (thread_.joinable()) thread_.join();
    }

    void fetch(const std::string& url, const std::map<std::string, std::string>& headers = {}, int timeout = 5000) {
        stop_ = false;
        auto request = cpr::GetCallback([this](cpr::Response r){ this->onReceive(r); }, [this](cpr::Response r){ this->onFail(r); });
        thread_ = std::thread(&DataFetchingEngine::fetchAsync, this, url, headers, timeout);
    }

    void stop() {
        stop_ = true;
    }

private:
    bool stop_{false};
    std::thread thread_;

    void fetchAsync(const std::string& url, const std::map<std::string, std::string>& headers, int timeout) {
        cpr::Session session;
        session.SetUrl(url);
        session.SetTimeout(timeout);
        for (auto & header : headers) {
            session.Header(header.first, header.second);
        }

        auto response = session.Get();
        std::cout << "Response: " << response.text << std::endl;  // For simplicity, print the response to console. Ideally parse it using a suitable JSON parser.
    }

    void onReceive(cpr::Response r) {
        if (r.error.code != cpr::ErrorCode::OK || stop_) return;

        rapidjson::Document document;
        if (!document.Parse(r.text.c_str()).HasParseError()) { // If the data is in JSON format, parse it.
            std::cout << "Received JSON data!" << std::endl; 
        } else { // If not a JSON string, assume CSV or XML and handle accordingly.
            std::cout << "Unrecognized Data Format" << std::endl;
        }
    }

    void onFail(cpr::Response r) {
        if (stop_) return;

        // Error Handling here
    }
};
```

This module is a simple asynchronous HTTP client using the `libcurl` library. It uses `rapidjson` for parsing JSON data, but it could be easily adapted to support other formats like CSV and XML. The `fetch` function starts an asynchronous operation in another thread that can be stopped by calling `stop()`.

The actual implementation will depend on the specific requirements of your application, such as the handling of different types of data formats and error recovery strategies. Additionally, this is a very basic implementation without any caching or concurrency control mechanisms, which would need to be implemented for production use. 

Finally, this code doesn't include any kind of configuration management (loading from env vars or config files), which you would need to implement based on your specific needs.
']

