void print_truncated(const char* str, size_t max_size) {
    if (strlen(str) > max_size) {
        // The string is longer than the limit, so we need to truncate it.
        char truncated[max_size + 1];  // +1 for null termination character

        memcpy(truncated, str, max_size);
        truncated[max_size] = '\0';  // Null-terminate the string

        printf("%s\n", truncated);
    } else {
        printf("%s\n", str);
    }
}
```
This function takes a string and a size limit as input. If the string's length is greater than the specified limit, it trims it to fit within that limit by copying only up to `max_size` characters from the original string into a new buffer. Afterward, it prints the (possibly) truncated string.
