['Creating a full solution for the given problem is beyond the capabilities of this platform. However, I can provide you with code snippets that show how certain features might be implemented in C++. 

1. Dynamic Update Logic:
```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <algorithm>

std::forward_list<std::string> cache;
std::unordered_map<std::string, std::forward_list<std::string>::iterator> mapCache;
std::forward_list<std::string> list;

void processStringList(const std::string& searchStr, const std::string& updateStr) {
    if (auto it = mapCache.find(searchStr); it != mapCache.end()) {  // Cache hit
        // Move the found element to the front of cache for LRU replacement policy
        auto elemIt = *mapCache[searchStr];
        if(elemIt != list.begin()) {
            list.splice_after(list.cbefore_begin(), list, elemIt); 
            mapCache[*elemIt] = list.begin(); // Update the iterator in cache
        } 
    } else {  
        auto it = std::find(list.begin(), list.end(), searchStr);
        if (it == list.end()) { // Cache miss, append to list
            list.push_front(updateStr);
            mapCache[updateStr] = list.begin();

            // Remove the last element from cache for LRU replacement policy
            if(mapCache.size() > CACHE_SIZE) { 
                mapCache.erase(list.back());
                list.pop_back();
            }
        } else {  // Found in list but not in cache, update the cache
            if(mapCache.size() == CACHE_SIZE) {  
                mapCache.erase(list.back());
                list.pop_back();
            }
            list.splice_after(list.cbefore_begin(), list, it); 
            mapCache[searchStr] = list.begin(); // Update the iterator in cache
        }
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a simple implementation of an LRU cache and it's trade-off between space and time complexity. The time complexity for search operation is O(1) which is achieved by using unordered map but it may have higher memory usage compared to Least Frequently Used (LFU) or Most Recently Used (MRU) caching policies which require more complex data structures.

2. Logging Framework: 
