['This is a complex task that cannot be fully solved within this text generation model's context because it requires designing a complete microservice architecture from scratch. However, I will provide you with a basic skeleton of how you could start developing the `DynamicTaskProcessor` service in C using event-driven programming and asynchronous tasks processing.

Please note that creating such a system from scratch is quite extensive, and would likely require a team of developers working across several weeks to months. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Structure for task data
typedef struct {
    char* command; // Shell command to execute
    void* payload; // Task-specific payload
    void (*callback)(int, void*); // Callback function
} TaskData;

// Thread routine that executes shell commands
void *executeTask(void *arg) {
    TaskData *task = (TaskData*) arg;

    FILE *fp = popen(task->command, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) return NULL;

    char result[256];
    while (fgets(result, sizeof(result), fp) != NULL) {
        // Process command output here
    }

    int status = pclose(fp);
    if (status == -1) {
        // Handle error
    } else {
        task->callback(status, task->payload);
    }
}

// Function to schedule a new task
void scheduleTask(char* command, void* payload, void (*callback)(int, void*)) {
    TaskData *task = malloc(sizeof(TaskData));
    task->command = strdup(command); // Make a copy of the command string
    task->payload = payload;
    task->callback = callback;

    pthread_t thread;
    if (pthread_create(&thread, NULL, executeTask, task) != 0) {
        // Handle error
        free(task);
    } else {
        // The task has been scheduled and will be executed in a separate thread
    }
}
```
This is a very basic start to creating your microservice. It creates a `TaskData` structure that holds a shell command, some payload, and a callback function that gets called when the task completes. A new thread is created for each scheduled task, which executes the task in the background without blocking.

The code does not include many of the features you described such as dynamic resource allocation, error handling, or integration with external services. It's also missing the key aspects of microservice architecture like load balancing, service discovery, and health checking. All these would need to be implemented separately and integrated into your system. 

Again, this is a very simplistic approach and does not consider many of the factors you mentioned in detail, such as error handling, integration with external services, load balancing etc. Such a complex system should ideally be broken down into smaller tasks using Agile methodologies and iteratively built up. It's likely that several different team members would work on each part of this system over time. 

This is not an easy task to complete from scratch in a single text response, but hopefully it gives you a starting point for your own development journey.
']

