    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void extendStateList(int value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Create new node
    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->value = value;
    newNode->next = head;

    // Update the head of the list
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code provides a simple linked list with thread-safe insertion. It uses `pthread_mutex_t` for mutual exclusion when modifying the state, ensuring that only one thread can modify the shared state (the linked list) at any given time. 

Please note that this is a very simplified example and does not cover many aspects of your requirements such as integration with cloud services, real-time processing, distributed processing, error handling, performance optimization etc. These are complex topics which require a much more thorough design and implementation.
