['Creating such a complex system as described would be quite an extensive piece of code, but I can provide you with a simplified example using C and some concepts mentioned in your problem description. Note this is only one part of the whole solution, not the entire microservice. 

Let's assume that we have a file named "task_processor.h":

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <errno.h>

typedef struct {
    char command[256];
    void *payload; // replace with actual payload type if needed
} task_t;

void executeTask(task_t task);
```
And a file named "task_processor.c":

```c
#include "task_processor.h"

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

int executeShellCommand(char* command) {
    char buff[256];
    FILE* fp;

    /* Open the command for reading. */
    fp = popen(command, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to run command\n" );
        return -1;
    }

    /* Read the output a line at a time - output it. */
    while (fgets(buff, sizeof(buff)-1, fp)!=NULL) {
        printf("%s", buff);
    }

    /* close */
    pclose(fp);
    return 0;
}

void* handleTaskThread(void *vargp) {
    task_t *task = (task_t *) vargp;
    executeShellCommand(task->command);
    free(task);
    return NULL;
}

int createNewThreadForTask(task_t task) {
    pthread_t thread_id;

    if (pthread_create(&thread_id, NULL, handleTaskThread, &task)) {
        printf("Failed to create new thread\n");
        return -1;
    }
    return 0;
}

void executeTask(task_t task) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    int error = createNewThreadForTask(task);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    if (error != 0) {
        printf("Failed to handle task\n");
    } else {
        printf("Successfully handled task\n");
    }
}
```
This is a simple example of how you could create threads in C to execute tasks. It uses a mutex for synchronization, which ensures that only one thread can access the code section at any time. 

Please note: This is a very simplified example and doesn't cover many aspects like error handling, memory management, task priority, etc. The complexity of your microservice greatly depends on your specific requirements. Consider using a framework or language which provides built-in support for these features to ease development. For instance, in Python, libraries like asyncio and multiprocessing can be used to manage tasks concurrently and execute them asynchronously.
']

["This is an extensive task involving multiple components including data ingestion, resource management, scalability & fault tolerance, advanced querying, security and compliance, RESTful API, monitoring & logging, testing & validation, documentation, and continuous improvement. Due to the complexity of this project, here's a simplified implementation for octree structure in C++ with some basic functionalities.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
const int DIM = 3; // Change it based on your dimensions (2 or 3)
struct Point {
    double x, y, z;
};
class OctreeNode {
public:
    Point pos;
    double size;
    vector<OctreeNode*> children;
    void createChildren() {
        for(int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
            OctreeNode* child = new OctreeNode();
            child->size = size / 2.0;
            double halfSize = child->size / 2.0;
            child->pos.x = pos.x + ((i & 4)? halfSize : -halfSize);
            child->pos.y = pos.y + ((i & 2)? halfSize : -halfSize);
            child->pos.z = pos.z + ((i & 1)? halfSize : -halfSize);
            children.push_back(child);
        }
    }
};
class Octree {
public:
    OctreeNode* root;
    double size;
    int capacity;
    Octree(double sz, int cap) {
        size = sz;
        capacity = cap;
        root = new OctreeNode();
        root->size = size / 2.0;
        root->pos.x = root->pos.y = root->pos.z = size / 2.0;
    }
    void insert(Point p) {
        if (root->children.size() < capacity) {
            // Insert the point to node
        } else {
            if(!root->children.empty()) {
