["The task you're asking for is quite complex, so I will provide a simplified version of the disjoint set data structure in Rust. The actual implementation would require a lot more considerations such as network partitions, machine learning model for API performance prediction, etc., which are beyond the scope of this response. 

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

pub struct DisjointSet<T> {
    pub nodes: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<T, T>>>,
}

impl<T: Eq + Ord + Clone + std::hash::Hash>  DisjointSet<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        DisjointSet{nodes: Arc::new(Mutex::new(HashMap::new()))}
    }

    pub fn make_set(&self, x: T) {
        let mut nodes = self.nodes.lock().unwrap();
        if !nodes.contains_key(&x){
            nodes.insert(x.clone(), x);
        }
    }

    pub fn find(&self, x: &T) -> Option<&T> {
        let mut nodes = self.nodes.lock().unwrap();
        if let Some(&p) = nodes.get(x){
            if p == *x{
                return Some(&p);
            } else {
                return self.find(&p);
            }
        }
        None
    }

    pub fn union(&self, x: &T, y: &T) -> Option<&T> {
        let root_x = self.find(x)?;
        let root_y = self.find(y)?;

        if root_x != root_y{
            let mut nodes = self.nodes.lock().unwrap();
            *nodes.get_mut(&root_y).unwrap() = root_x.clone();
        }
        Some(root_x)
    }
}
``` 

This implementation is a simplified version of the disjoint set data structure with find and union operations, where all nodes are stored in a HashMap and mutation to the data structure is protected by a Mutex for thread safety. 

Please note that this is just a start, and it doesn't cover everything you asked for (like handling network partitions or machine learning models for API performance prediction). For the full solution, I would recommend breaking down the task into smaller tasks and tackling them one at a time.
"]

