['This is an extensive task and would require a significant amount of code to fully implement it, as well as a detailed understanding of your specific use case. However, I will provide a simplified version of the class you requested.

For this simplified example, we'll make use of Netty, which is a high-performance event-driven network application framework that can be used for building large-scale non-blocking servers.

```java
import io.netty.channel.ChannelHandlerContext;
import io.netty.handler.codec.MessageToMessageDecoder;
import org.apache.commons.io.IOUtils;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.List;

public class StreamingEventProcessor extends MessageToMessageDecoder<ByteBuf> {
    @Override
    protected void decode(ChannelHandlerContext ctx, ByteBuf msg, List<Object> out) throws Exception {
        byte[] bytes = new byte[msg.readableBytes()];
        msg.getBytes(0, bytes);

        // Parse and validate the event based on its format (JSON, XML, binary)
        Event event;
        String data = new String(bytes, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
        if (isJson(data)) {
            event = parseFromJson(data);
        } else if (isXml(data)) {
            event = parseFromXml(data);
        } else {
            event = parseFromBinary(bytes);
        }

        // If the parsing or validation fails, log and discard the event
        if (!event.isValid()) {
            LoggerFactory.getLogger().error("Invalid event: " + data);
            return;
        }

        out.add(event);
    }
}
```
In a real-world application, you'd also need to handle error handling and retries for transient issues with external services. You'll likely want to use a robust event processing framework like Apache Flink or Apache Kafka Streams for this kind of workload as they provide built-in support for fault tolerance, scalability, and exactly-once semantics.

