['Here's a very high-level pseudocode representation of how such a utility might look like in Java. Note that actual implementation would require additional error handling, logging, configuration settings, etc. Also note that this example doesn't take into account all mentioned requirements but it's an illustrative starting point:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class PersistentObjectLoader<T> {

    private static final Logger LOGGER = Logger.getLogger(PersistentObjectLoader.class.getName());
    private final ExecutorService executor;
    private final Path serializedDataPath;
    // Add more fields for version compatibility, checksum verification and error handling...

    public PersistentObjectLoader(ExecutorService executor, Path serializedDataPath) {
        this.executor = executor;
        this.serializedDataPath = serializedDataPath;
        // Initialize other fields here...
    }

    public Future<T> load() {
        return executor.submit(() -> {
            try {
                byte[] data = Files.readAllBytes(serializedDataPath);

                // Verify checksum or hash value, handle version compatibility and perform other verifications...

                ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
                ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais);
                @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
                T obj = (T) ois.readObject();
                return obj;
            } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
                LOGGER.log(Level.SEVERE, "Deserialization failed", e);
                throw new RuntimeException("Deserialization failed", e);
            }
        });
    }
}
```
The `load` method is asynchronous and returns a `Future<T>` which can be used to retrieve the loaded object. The actual network I/O, checksum verification and version compatibility handling are performed in separate threads managed by the provided executor service.

Please note that for this simple example all error handling is kept very basic, actual implementation should include more sophisticated mechanisms for dealing with potential issues like network errors, transient failures, invalid data etc. Also, proper integration testing needs to be done to ensure all edge cases are covered. This would require a test suite and potentially mocks for the network layer, file system or blockchain-based data integrity service. 

Remember that Java serialization is not designed for long term storage, it's often better to use other formats like JSON/XML if you need long term persistence. Also please note that concurrency control should be carefully managed when dealing with shared resources and especially when dealing with mutable objects (like ArrayLists).
']

