["Creating such a complex system would require many classes and functions that might not fit within this textbox due to character limit. Instead, here are some of the key elements that you'd need to include in your DynamicStringListManager class:

```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <chrono>
#include <unordered_map>

class DynamicStringListManager {
private:
    std::forward_list<std::string> list;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> cache;  // Assuming the search string is part of a substring
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    bool found = false;

public:
    void insert(const std::string& searchString, const std::string& insertionString) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        if (cache.find(searchString) != cache.end()) {
            // The search string was previously found and cached
            auto it = list.begin();
            std::advance(it, cache[searchString]);

            // Insert the insertion string at the optimal position
            list.insert_after(it, insertionString);
        } else {
            for (auto it = list.before_begin(), nextIt = list.begin(); 
                 nextIt != list.end(); ++it, ++nextIt) {
                if (*nextIt.find(searchString) != std::string::npos) {
                    // The search string was found in a substring of an existing element
                    cache[searchString] = std::distance(list.begin(), nextIt);

                    // Insert the insertion string at this position
                    list.insert_after(it, insertionString);
                    found = true;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (!found) {
                // The search string was not found anywhere in the list
                list.push_front(insertionString);
            }
        }

        cv.notify_all();
    }

    bool search(const std::string& searchString) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        while (!found) {
            cv.wait_for(lock, std::chrono::seconds(5));  // Timeout after 5 seconds

            if (found) { break; }
        }

        return found;
    }
};
```
This is just a very simplified version of what your `DynamicStringListManager` class could look like. It uses mutexes and condition variables to ensure thread safety, and also implements a cache for previously searched strings to optimize search times.

For logging, you would need to use an external API or library for handling log entries, such as spdlog or glog. For system dependencies and API integration, consider using libraries like cpr or Poco::Net for network operations. You could also leverage a thread pooling mechanism like Intel TBB's `tbb::concurrent_unordered_map` for concurrency.
"]

