["This task involves designing an advanced middleware component that integrates with multiple Kafka clusters and manages real-time event streams and batch processing within a dynamic microservices architecture. Here's a simplified version of such a codebase (please note that actual production-level code would be much more complex):

```csharp
public class MiddlewareComponent : IMiddlewareComponent
{
    private IEnumerable<IKafkaCluster> clusters;

    public void Initialize(IEnumerable<IKafkaCluster> kafkaClusters)
    {
        clusters = kafkaClusters;
    }

    // Method for managing dynamic cluster configurations
    public void ManageDynamicConfigurations()
    {
        foreach (var cluster in clusters)
        {
            if (!cluster.IsActive())
                HandleInactiveCluster(cluster);
            else 
                HandleActiveCluster(cluster);
        }
    }

    // Method for managing leader elections and partition reassignments
    private void HandleActiveCluster(IKafkaCluster cluster)
    {
        cluster.LeaderElection();
        cluster.Reassignment();
    }

    // Method for handling inactive clusters
    private void HandleInactiveCluster(IKafkaCluster cluster)
    {
        cluster.MarkAsInactive();
        RedistributeWorkloads(cluster);
    }

    // Method to redistribute workloads across active clusters
    private void RedistributeWorkloads(IKafkaCluster inactiveCluster)
    {
        var activeClusters = clusters.Where(c => c.IsActive());

        foreach (var cluster in activeClusters)
            cluster.RedistributeWorkloadFromInactiveCluster(inactiveCluster);
    }

    // Other methods for advanced deduplication, conflict resolution and versioning are implemented here...
}
```
This is a simplified skeleton of the middleware component. Please note that each of these operations would require their own classes and interfaces to handle specific functionalities such as leader election, reassignments, conflict resolution, etc. 

For example, a `HandleInactiveCluster()` method could be implemented in the `KafkaCluster` class using an event-driven pattern:
```csharp
public void HandleInactiveCluster()
{
    if (!this.IsActive())
        this.InvokeOnClusterFailureEvent();
}

private void InvokeOnClusterFailureEvent()
{
    OnClusterFailure?.Invoke(this, new ClusterFailureEventArgs());
}
```
And the `MiddlewareComponent` would subscribe to that event:
```csharp
public MiddlewareComponent()
{
    foreach (var cluster in clusters)
        cluster.OnClusterFailure += HandleClusterFailure;
}

private void HandleClusterFailure(object sender, ClusterFailureEventArgs args)
{
    var cluster = sender as KafkaCluster;
    if (!cluster.IsActive())
        RedistributeWorkloads(cluster);
}
```
This is just one way to handle cluster failures and adapt to changes in configurations. Other approaches could involve polling, reactive programming with Rx.NET, etc., depending on the specific requirements of your system. 

