['Your requirements involve complex systems development with multiple features and aspects. The integration of DNS resolution service with an existing application infrastructure, handling distributed key-value store updates, and integrating it with other components in a system can be quite complex due to the sheer number of variables that need to be handled - like resource management, load balancing, error handling, logging etc.

Coding this from scratch would be a large task and beyond the scope of this platform. However, I will provide a high level pseudocode-like solution outline for your requirements:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

/* Structures to store hostname -> IP mapping */
typedef struct {
    char *host;
    char *ipv4;
    char *ipv6;
} HostEntry;

/* Global variables for DNS resolution service configuration */
int query_timeout = 5000; /* Default timeout in milliseconds */
char* remote_servers[10] = { NULL }; 
float server_priority[10] = { 0.0f };

pthread_mutex_t cache_lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; /* Mutex for thread safety */
/* Structure to store cached hostname -> IP mapping */
HostEntry *cache[1024] = { NULL }; 
size_t cache_capacity = 1024; /* Default cache size */
size_t cache_current_size = 0; /* Current size of the cache */

/* Function to handle DNS resolution request */
void* resolveDNS(void *arg) {
    char* hostname = (char*) arg;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&cache_lock);
    HostEntry *entry = NULL; 

    /* Check cache for entry, if found return it. */
    int i = 0;
    while (i < cache_capacity && !entry) {
        if(cache[i] && strcmp(cache[i]->host, hostname) == 0){
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&cache_lock);
            return entry;
        }
        i++;
    }

    /* If not found in cache, fetch from remote server */
    entry = fetchFromRemoteServer(hostname); 

    /* Add the fetched entry to cache. */
    if(cache_current_size < cache_capacity){
        cache[cache_current_size++] = entry;
    } else {
        /* If cache is full, evict least recently used item */
        HostEntry* leastRecentlyUsedItem = getLeastRecentlyUsed(); 
        replaceCacheEntry(leastRecentlyUsedItem, entry);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&cache_lock);
    return entry;
}

int main() {
    /* Initialize hostname -> IP mapping */
    HostEntry* mappings = initHostMappings(); 

    /* Create threads for concurrent DNS resolutions */
    pthread_t threads[10];
    int i = 0;
    while (i < 10) {
        if(pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, resolveDNS, mappings[i].host)){
            printf("Error creating thread\n");
            return 1;
        }
        i++;
    }

    /* Join all threads */
    i = 0;
    while (i < 10) {
        if(pthread_join(threads[i], NULL)) {
            printf("Error joining thread\n");
            return 2;
        }
        i++;
    }

    /* Clean up */
    freeHostMappings(mappings);

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that this is a very simplistic implementation and does not cover all the aspects you requested like server priority, configurable cache size, retry logic with exponential backoff etc. For these features, I would recommend using an existing library or framework which already has these functionalities built in such as `libuv` for C, which can provide a high-performance event loop and asynchronous IO. The solution provided here is only meant to illustrate the basic structure of how you might approach this problem with C programming.
']

