['Writing this entire application from scratch is out of scope due to its complexity, but I can show you a simplified example for the memory management part in C++ using `std::vector` for dynamic allocation and RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) idiom for automatic deallocation.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <vector>

// Define a wrapper around std::vector with thread-safe interface.
class MemoryPool {
public:
    void* allocate(size_t size) {
        // Lock the mutex before accessing shared data.
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mMutex);

        if (mPool.capacity() - mPool.size() < size) {
            std::cout << "Reallocating memory pool\n";

            // Double the capacity to avoid frequent reallocation.
            mPool.reserve(std::max(2 * size, 2 * mPool.capacity()));
        }

        void* ptr = &mPool[mPool.size()];
        mPool.resize(mPool.size() + size);
        return ptr;
    }

private:
    std::mutex mMutex;
    std::vector<char> mPool;
};

// Global memory pool.
MemoryPool gMemPool;

void workerThread(int id) {
    void* ptr = gMemPool.allocate(1024);  // Allocate 1 KB of memory.

    std::cout << "Thread " << id << " allocated at: " << ptr << '\n';
}

int main() {
    constexpr int numThreads = 16;
    std::thread threads[numThreads];

    for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; ++i) {
        threads[i] = std::thread(workerThread, i);
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }
}
```
In this example, a `MemoryPool` class is used to manage memory allocation in a thread-safe manner. The `allocate()` function allocates memory from the pool and returns a pointer to it. This function locks a mutex before accessing shared data to ensure that no other threads can modify the pool at the same time, thus ensuring thread safety.

The `workerThread()` function simulates work by allocating some amount of memory. Multiple threads are created in the main function, each allocated different amounts of memory, demonstrating how the memory pool handles concurrent accesses. 

Remember that this is a simplified example and real-world scenarios would be more complex including considerations for memory alignment, defragmentation, garbage collection etc. And also, handling high cardinality indexes, partitioning strategies, data deduplication and transactions can be challenging in multi-threaded environment. It's recommended to use existing mature database management libraries instead of building these features from scratch.
']

