void handle_timeout(int timeout, pthread_mutex_t *lock){
    for(int i=1;i<=timeout;i++){ 
        sleep(1); // Pause the current thread for 1 second before checking again.

        if(!pthread_mutex_trylock(lock)) {
            return;
        }
    }

    /* If we reach here, it means that we have exhausted all timeout */
}

bool oauthValidateAndAuthenticate(SecurityContext* sc){
    static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;  // Initialize a mutex for thread-safety.

    if (pthread_mutex_lock(&lock) != 0) {  // Lock the mutex to prevent race conditions.
        perror("Failed to lock");
        return false;
    }

    /* Password validation */
    if (!validate_password(sc->password)) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // Unlock the mutex before returning.
        return false;
    }

    /* Timeout handling */
    handle_timeout(sc->timeout, &lock);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // Remember to always unlock the mutex after you're done with it.
    return true;
}
```
In this example, I have used a simple password validation function and timeout handling mechanism that involves sleeping for one second at a time until either the operation completes or the timeout expires. Please note that actual OAuth implementation will be more complex. Also, don't forget to handle error scenarios properly - you should return meaningful errors, log them and possibly retry certain operations.
